  "answer_text": "When we refer to \"clean\" or \"pure\" gold, we are talking about **fine gold** (commonly known as 24-karat gold), which is stamped with a purity of **99.9% (999 fine)** or **99.99% (999.9 fine)**. \n\nIt is physically, economically, and scientifically impossible to produce **100% pure gold**. The purity of refined gold tops out at 99.9% or 99.99% due to several factors:\n\n### 1. Technical and Chemical Limits of Refining\nRefining raw gold into pure gold is a multi-step chemical process. Most industrial refiners use two main methods:\n* **The Miller Process:** This uses gaseous chlorine to filter out impurities, bringing the gold's purity up to about 99.5%. \n* **The Wohlwill Process:** An advanced electrochemical process that refines the gold further, typically reaching 99.9% or 99.99% purity.\n\nAt a microscopic level, certain foreign elements (like silver, copper, platinum, iron, or lead) share highly similar chemical behaviors with gold. Forcing these final, stubborn trace molecules out of the gold lattice is exceptionally difficult, meaning some minute impurities will always remain trapped.\n\n### 2. The Law of Diminishing Returns (Exponential Cost)\nIn chemistry, purifying a substance from 90% to 99% is relatively straightforward. However, as you approach 100%, the effort and energy required do not increase linearly\u2014they increase **exponentially**. \nTo remove the absolute last fraction of a percent of impurities to reach a theoretical 100% would require highly controlled, laboratory-grade environments, specialized chemical reagents, and an immense amount of time. The astronomical cost of doing this would far exceed the market value of the gold itself, making it economically unfeasible.\n\n### 3. Scientific and Measurement Limits\nTo declare something is \"100% pure,\" a laboratory must be able to prove it. However, even the most advanced scientific measuring instruments (such as Mass Spectrometers or X-ray fluorescence analyzers) have **detection limits**. They cannot guarantee the absence of a single foreign atom. Stating \"99.9%\" or \"99.99%\" is a scientifically accurate and legally safe claim, confirming that no impurities were detected beyond the instrument's tiny threshold. \n\n### 4. Immediate Environmental Contamination\nEven if a refinery somehow achieved 100% pure gold in a vacuum, the second that gold is exposed to the real world, its purity would drop. Pouring the molten gold into a mold, exposing it to the air, or even touching it with tools introduces microscopic contaminants like carbon, oxygen, dust, or trace oils from human skin. \n\n***\n\n**Fun Fact:** The closest humanity has ever gotten to \"perfect\" gold was achieved in 1957 by Australia's Perth Mint, which produced a plate of gold with a purity of **99.9999%** (often called \"six nines\"). Even at this world-record level of extreme refinement, a tiny 0.0001% of the plate was still made of other elements.",
